            <ArticleTitle>Effects of the antagomiRs 15b and 200b on the altered healing pattern of diabetic mice.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/bph.14113</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Diabetic subjects with non-healing ulcers have a reduced expression of vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF). Genetically diabetic mice have an altered expression pattern of VEGF and its receptor, VEGFR-2. In diabetic wounds, miR15b and miR200b which respectively inhibit VEGF and VEGF-R2 mRNAs are up-regulated, further affecting the impaired angiogenesis. We investigated whether anti-miRs directed toward miR15b and miR200b could improve wound repair in genetically diabetic mice.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="EXPERIMENTAL APPROACH" NlmCategory="METHODS">Wounds were produced on the back skin of female diabetic mice, animals received in the wound edge, the anti-miRs (antimiR15b, antimiR200b, or antimiR15b/200b) at the dose of 10mg/Kg, or vehicle. Mice were killed on day 7, 14 and at time of complete wound closure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="KEY RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">mRNA expression of VEGF, VEGFR-2, Angiopoietin-1, and its receptor TEK were evaluated after 7 and 14 days. Protein levels of VEGF and transglutaminase II were measured at day 7, while VEGFR-2 and Angiopoietin-1 at day 14. Histological features and the time to achieve a complete wound closure were also examined. Treatment with the anti-miRs improved the analyzed parameters and the co-treatment resulted the most effective.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION AND IMPLICATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The results suggest that the inhibition of miR15b and miR200b may have a potential application in diabetes-related wound disorders.</AbstractText>
